WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
A shell script should receive secrets through the narrowest practical channel.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.440
That means the secret is not placed in the script just because it is convenient.

3
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:24.300
It is delivered at runtime through a mechanism appropriate for the environment: a managed secret store, workload identity, platform-managed identity, CI/CD secret variable, protected file, restricted file descriptor, short-lived token, or another approved pattern.

4
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.720
The exact mechanism changes, but the design principle stays the same.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.280
The script should expose the value only to the job that needs it, for only as long as needed.

6
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:42.380
Avoid passing secrets to unrelated tools, broad child processes, debugging wrappers, or commands that may record their inputs.

7
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.640
If a script launches other processes, think about what those processes inherit.

8
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:52.580
A secret needed by one command does not need to become ambient state for every later command in the workflow.

9
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.420
Runtime checks should also be careful.

10
00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.960
It is reasonable for a script to verify that a required secret is available before continuing.

11
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:08.340
It is not reasonable to print the value as part of a check, echo it in a debug trace, or include it in a failure message.

12
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:14.840
A safe failure message should tell the operator what is missing in operational terms without revealing the secret.

13
00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:22.460
For example, the script can say that a required deployment token was not provided through the approved channel, without displaying the token value.

14
00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.380
Short-lived credentials are especially useful where the platform supports them.

15
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.040
They reduce the value of stale copies and make cleanup easier when a job completes.

16
00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.940
Still, short-lived does not mean careless.

17
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.360
A token with broad privileges can cause damage even during a short lifetime.

18
00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:46.100
Pair short lifetime with least privilege, controlled injection, minimal inheritance, and useful audit records.

19
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.920
A non-sensitive preflight check verifies that the required secret is available through the approved channel before work begins.
